E15-S6|E15|Logs, alarms and cost guard are real|ops|E15-S4|verified|A6-verify|feat/E15-S6-observability|cc89a1f|evidence/E15-S6.md|2026-10-29T04:10Z|orchestrator|Orchestrator applied the plan (11 add / 1 change / 0 destroy) after removing the drift cause first: ecs.tf and portal.tf now carry ignore_changes on container_definitions and on the service task_definition, so the pipeline owns image revisions and an unrelated apply can no longer roll a deploy back. Before that fix the same plan destroyed three task definitions. Clause 1 PASS, verified from live task definitions not HCL: api/worker/portal awslogs-group are /ecs/evia-beta/{api,worker,portal}, api last event 11 min old, portal 16 min, worker 6.8 h (idle and silent, which is why a log-based alarm cannot cover it). Clause 2 PASS: 8 alarms live, 3 of them running-tasks-low for api/worker/portal plus rds-connections-high, rds-free-storage-low and the 3 pre-existing; worker went INSUFFICIENT_DATA -> OK on real datapoints with threshold 1.0. Clause 3 PASS: budget evia-beta-monthly 250 USD with a Confirmed email subscriber; the ops-alerts email subscription remains PendingConfirmation so alarms also feed an SQS sink. Also landed: ALB access logs, verified by objects arriving at s3://evia-beta-alb-logs-562654496378/alb/AWSLogs/... within 2 minutes. terraform plan -detailed-exitcode = 0, no drift. Cost +$0.61/mo. Separate finding recorded as E15-S8: Container Insights is enabled and was absent from the cost model at roughly $32/mo.
E15-S7|E15|Docs-only commits do not redeploy the stack|build|E15-S2|verified|A10-ci|feat/E15-S7-docs-no-deploy|d56b5c4|evidence/E15-S7.md|2026-10-30T04:00Z|orchestrator|VERIFIED by the orchestrator on the live pipeline, both halves, in the order the criterion names. Mechanism: buildspec pre_build runs scripts/deploy-scope.py, which reads the commit the live services were built from out of ECS (each service's PRIMARY task definition pins the image tag, and IMAGE_TAG is the 12-char commit SHA) and diffs that tree against HEAD; every changed path inside docs/, evidence/ or plan/ yields skip, anything else yields deploy. The helper is authorised to write exactly one token and only the literal string 'skip' is accepted; every other outcome - helper crash, missing file, garbage, ECS unreachable, failures[], mid-rollout, services on different tags, tag not a SHA, commit not fetchable - falls back to a full deploy. I re-ran the child's proof myself: scripts/verify-deploy-scope.py reports 32/32, including 12 cannot-tell cases that all deploy and a post_build harness proving the skip branch makes zero register-task-definition and zero update-service calls while the deploy branch makes 3 and 3. LIVE TWO-PUSH PROOF, run by me: (push 1, code) d56b5c4 changes buildspec.yml, scripts and infrastructure -> build f0f5b101 SUCCEEDED, api 31->32, worker 31->32, portal 29->30, all three rollout COMPLETED, /health commit d56b5c491b8b equals the image tag. (push 2, docs only) this ledger-and-evidence commit -> build skipped the rollout: no task-definition revision registered, api/worker/portal remain at 32/32/30, build still SUCCEEDED so the GitHub commit status stays green. ONE INFRASTRUCTURE CHANGE APPLIED BY ME, not the child: pipeline.tf git_clone_depth 1 -> 0 (targeted apply, 0 add / 1 change / 0 destroy, no task definition in the diff), read back from AWS as depth 0. At depth 1 the deployed commit is not in the clone and the helper must deepen mid-build, an unverified network path that fails closed; full history costs a few seconds of DOWNLOAD_SOURCE on a ~22 MB repo and removes the dependency. NOT DONE and deliberately so: image build, ECR push, scan and migrations still run on a docs-only commit, so the saving is the ECS rollout only - making those cheap too is a separate story. Root-level README.md, *.docx and QA/ are NOT in the allowlist and still deploy, which matches the acceptance as written. shellcheck is unavailable on this host, so the 48 buildspec blocks were checked with bash -n and the post_build harness instead.
E15-S8|E15|Container Insights replaced by a free task-stop alert path|build|E15-S6|verified|A-cost|feat/E15-S8-alerts|a671f42|evidence/E15-S8-apply.md|2026-10-29T16:07Z|orchestrator|VERIFIED by me on the live stack, not from the child's report. Plan reviewed before applying: 3 add, 1 change, 3 destroy, with NO task definition in the diff, so ignore_changes from E15-S6 held; applied with -var image_tag=6de0209c9dd9 because terraform.tfvars still pins an older tag and applying on the tfvars would have rolled all three services backwards. After apply, read back from AWS: five alarms remain and none is in ECS/ContainerInsights (api-target-5xx, api-unhealthy-hosts, portal-unhealthy-hosts, rds-connections-high, rds-free-storage-low); cluster containerInsights=disabled; rule evia-beta-task-stopped ENABLED with target arn:aws:sns:ap-south-1:562654496378:evia-beta-ops-alerts. End-to-end proof run by me with scripts/verify-e15s8-task-stop-alert.sh: throwaway task a93db3c3e4b245fc856083a42714c756 ran with exit 3, and the alert came back through EventBridge into the ops SQS sink in 49s with source aws.ecs, detail-type ECS Task State Change, lastStatus STOPPED, desiredStatus STOPPED, stopCode EssentialContainerExited, container exit codes [3]. DEFECT FOUND BY RUNNING IT: the first run crashed because the sink still holds CloudWatch alarm notifications from the E15-S6 proof at 03:41Z, whose SNS Message is an alarm document rather than an EventBridge event, and the reader parsed body[Message] unguarded - fixed in this commit (non-JSON, non-dict and detail-less messages are skipped). The child's rehearsal used an empty isolated queue and never met that case. Saving recorded as an ESTIMATE: 108 measured metric streams x 0.30/metric-month = 32.40 plus 3 alarms x 0.10 = 32.70/month (392.40/yr), because Cost Explorer shows 0.00 for the month and there is no invoice to difference. Given up knowingly: Container Insights was the only per-service CPU/memory source; AWS/ECS free metrics would restore that for about 1.20/month.
