E16-S7|E16|Security and DPDP checklist signed off|ops|E16-S3|in_review|A-security|feat/E16-S7-dpdp2|a671f42|evidence/E16-S7.md||orchestrator|Checklist rev 2 re-derived against the live stack (15:28:45Z-15:46:12Z, deployed image 0f54ff687210) and two of the three rev-1 failures are now observed fixed. I independently confirmed the two load-bearing claims: the RDS log group /aws/rds/instance/evia-beta-postgres/postgresql now carries retentionInDays=14 (put-retention-policy applied and re-read; every log group in the account now has a retention set), and the deletion path is real - the live api log carries an account_deleted event for the synthetic user 11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111 with a per-table erase breakdown, 24 rows across 22 tables to zero, at 15:39Z. Clause 5 therefore moves FAILED -> PASS, exercised over HTTPS against the DEPLOYED code this time rather than in-process. HELD IN REVIEW with three clauses open and none of them softened: (2) the real Google OAuth client secret is still in git history at 0a724b0 in every ref AND the repo is still public - that is E16-S8, the user's console click, not a repo change; (3) the literal 24-hour zero-hit scan is calendar-bound - measured expiry 2026-11-12T05:37:37Z, so PENDING-DATE not PASS, although every post-fix window scans clean (388 events, 0 hits); (4) clinical records (Turn, SessionSummary, JournalEntry, TherapistNote, MoodLog, Appointment) still have no retention period at all - a clinical and legal decision, documented in writing including the premigrate snapshot decision (retained until 2026-11-27T19:06Z as the migration rollback point, tagged with owner and review date). One follow-up for the orchestrator captured by the child: the RDS log group is not Terraform-managed (RDS creates it implicitly), so the 14-day policy would silently vanish on a rebuild unless the resource is declared and imported.
E16-S8|E16|Public-repo exposure closed and leaked credentials rotated|ops||in_review|orchestrator|develop|2246a1b|evidence/E16-S8.md|2026-10-30T06:40Z|orchestrator|ENGINEERING HALF DONE AND VERIFIED BY ME; the two actions that actually remediate the leak are owner console actions and remain open. What I verified personally, not from the child's report: scripts/secret-scan.py tree exits 0 on the real merged tree (1090 files via git ls-files); with a fake GOCSPX- token added to a TRACKED file it exits 1 and prints the masked finding; removing it returns to 0. scripts/verify-secret-scan.py reports 21/21 cases. History scan exits 0 (advisory) and reports 2 findings. buildspec pre_build now runs the tree scan with no shell no-op fallback (fail-closed) and the history scan wrapped so it can never fail the build — deliberately asymmetric, because a permanently-red gate for a pre-existing leak gets deleted within a week. MY OWN FIRST NEGATIVE CONTROL WAS WRONG and I am recording it: I injected an UNTRACKED file and the scanner returned 0. That is correct behaviour, not a defect — the scanner walks git ls-files because a CodeBuild source tree only ever contains committed content — but it means the gate protects commits, not a developer's local scratch files. TWO CORRECTIONS TO MY OWN BRIEF, both evidenced by the child and checkable: (1) there is NO Postgres password in history — all ~2,488 postgres:// occurrences across 563 commits are placeholders (***, [password], ${var}, %s); I had told the user one was there. (2) A second leak I did not know about: apps/mobile/android/app/google-services.json carries a Google API key (AIzaSy prefix, 39 chars) across 124 commits, with no live tip. STILL OPEN, ALL OWNER: make okuvu/Okuvu private (checked again this round: private false, visibility public); rotate the Google OAuth client secret; rotate the Google API key; and either push develop over the four refs that still carry the secret in their CURRENT tree (yashraj/298d261, feat/agents-15/c49b747 and their remote-tracking counterparts) or delete those branches. docs/SECRET-REMEDIATION.md has the step-by-step.
